# AGENTS.md

Briefing for AI agents (and new contributors) working in this Odoo project.
It was generated by `odx new`; edit it as the project grows.

- **Project**: {{project_name}}
- **Odoo version**: {{version}}
- **Tooling**: the `odx` CLI drives everything below — prefer it over calling
  `odoo-bin`, `pip` or `psql` by hand, so the config and addons path stay in sync.

## Layout

```
.odx.toml          Odoo version this project targets
compose.yml        PostgreSQL for local development (Docker Compose)
odoo.conf          Committed base config
odoo.conf.local    Generated, git-ignored; odx rewrites its addons_path — do not hand-edit
custom_addons/     Modules written for this project (your work goes here)
external_addons/   Third-party modules (OCA, vendors) — treat as read-only
.venv/             Python environment used by every odx command
.testing/sessions/ Run and test logs (see "Where the logs are")
```

**Odoo's own source is not in this project.** One checkout per version is shared by
every project on this machine, so nothing here weighs 1.2 GB. Print its path with:

```bash
odx store path          # e.g. /home/you/.cache/odx/odoo/{{version}}
```

Never patch Odoo core. If core behavior needs to change, override it from a module in
`custom_addons/` (inheritance, `_inherit`, monkey-patch in `__init__.py` as a last
resort) — and remember an edit there would hit **every** project sharing that version.

## Finding code

Searching this project only reaches *this project* — Odoo core lives outside it, so a
project-wide search will never return framework code. Search each explicitly:

```bash
ODOO=$(odx store path)

rg "_compute_display_name" custom_addons/            # this project (start here)
rg "def _compute_display_name" "$ODOO/odoo/"         # the framework itself
rg "class ResPartner" "$ODOO/odoo/addons/base/"      # the base module
rg "class SaleOrder" "$ODOO/addons/sale/"            # any other core addon
```

Mind the two addon roots in a checkout: the `base` module lives under
`$ODOO/odoo/addons/`, every other standard module under `$ODOO/addons/`.

Do read core when you need the API you are extending: it is the authoritative
reference **for this exact Odoo version**, and checking it beats recalling how Odoo
behaved in a different one. Just never edit it.

If your tools refuse to read that path, that is an access boundary, not a missing
file: the store is outside the project directory. `odx new` writes it into
`.claude/settings.json` (`permissions.additionalDirectories`) for Claude Code; for
other agents, grant the path printed by `odx store path`. `odx doctor` reports whether
the grant is in place.

## Commands

```bash
odx db start                  # start PostgreSQL (docker compose)
odx run                       # run the server (live log dashboard)
odx run --plain               # plain streamed logs; use this when you need pdb
odx shell -d <db>             # Odoo shell against a database
odx update -d <db>            # update all modules
odx update-module <m> -d <db> # update one module
odx test [<tags>...]          # run tests in a throwaway database
odx i18n -d <db> [-m <mod>] [--lang es_BO]   # export .pot/.po per addon
odx install                   # fetch Odoo into the store + install Python deps
odx store ls                  # Odoo versions shared by all projects on this machine
odx clean                     # drop __pycache__/*.pyc/*.log, prune old run sessions
odx doctor                    # check system dependencies
odx db ls|psql|logs|stop      # database utilities
odx db drop <db> [--force]    # delete a database
```

Global flags worth knowing: `--json` (machine-readable output, no prompts/colors),
`--quiet` (errors and final results only), `--no-progress`, `--color never`.

## Running the server

`odx run` opens a live dashboard: `q` quit, `r` restart odoo-bin, `/` search,
`l` cycle the level filter, `g`/`G` top/bottom, arrows or `j`/`k` scroll, PageUp/
PageDown by a screenful, mouse wheel to scroll, Ctrl+C quits from anywhere.

While you are scrolled up the view stops following new lines and the status bar says
so (`paused -N lines`); `G` resumes tailing. Mouse capture is what makes the wheel
work, and it takes over the terminal's own text selection — press `m` to hand the
mouse back when you want to select and copy a traceback (many terminals also let you
select with Shift held).

`r` stops odoo-bin (gracefully, then forcefully) and starts it again without leaving
the dashboard — the log scrollback, filter and search are kept, and everything lands
in the same `run.log`. The addons path is rebuilt on each restart, so this is the way
to pick up a **newly created module** in `custom_addons/`, a change to `odoo.conf`, or
a `--dev=reload` that stopped reacting. Restarting does not touch the database: use
`odx update-module <m> -d <db>` after changing models, views or data files.

Both the dashboard and `--plain` run Odoo with `--dev=all`.

## Debugging

Every Odoo process odx starts is debuggable: it opens a **DAP** (Debug Adapter
Protocol) listener on `127.0.0.1:5678`, so attach from VS Code/Cursor (the generated
`.vscode/launch.json` has the config), nvim-dap, or any DAP client. `odx run` shows
the address in the dashboard title.

- `breakpoint()` anywhere in your addon code breaks into the attached client — odx
  sets `PYTHONBREAKPOINT` for that. With no client attached it does nothing, so
  leaving one in during a restart will not wedge the server (but do not commit it).
- `odx test --debug-wait` / `odx run --debug-wait` hold startup until a client
  attaches, which is how you catch something that fails during boot or early in a test.
- `--debug-port N` moves the listener; if the default is busy (a second project) odx
  picks the next free port and prints the one in use.
- The listener survives Odoo restarting itself (`--dev=reload`, or a module install
  asking for a restart) and the dashboard's `r`. Re-attach after a restart.
- It listens on loopback only, and the debugger never blocks Odoo from starting: if
  debugpy is missing (`odx install` installs it), odx logs a warning and runs on.

## Testing

```bash
odx test                                  # all tests of every module in custom_addons
odx test /my_module                       # one module
odx test /my_module:TestClass.test_method # one test
```

`odx test` creates a temporary database, installs the modules under test, runs them
in a single `odoo-bin` invocation and drops the database afterwards — including on
failure or Ctrl+C. Write tests with `TransactionCase`/`HttpCase` and tag them so they
can be selected individually.

## Where the logs are

- `.testing/sessions/run-<timestamp>/run.log` — full, unfiltered log of one `odx run`
  (written even when the dashboard filters what is on screen).
- `.testing/sessions/<run_id>/` — one `odx test` session: `combined.log`,
  `warnings.log`, `runs/*.log`, `report.json`, plus a `latest` symlink.

Read these instead of re-running a long command to see what happened. `odx clean`
keeps the five most recent `run-*` sessions and leaves test sessions alone.

## Module conventions

- One concern per module; technical names in `snake_case`.
- Required files: `__init__.py`, `__manifest__.py`, `security/ir.model.access.csv`
  for every new model.
- Code, identifiers and comments in English; user-facing strings go through `_()`
  and are translated in `i18n/*.po`.
- Keep `depends` minimal and accurate — it defines install and upgrade order.
- XML data files: use explicit external IDs, and `noupdate="1"` for records users
  are expected to edit.

Check the view syntax for **Odoo {{version}}** before writing XML: `<list>` replaced
`<tree>` in 18.0, and `attrs`/`states` were removed in 17.0 in favor of direct
`invisible`/`readonly`/`required` attributes with a Python expression.

## Agent briefing files

`AGENTS.md` (this file) is the single briefing. `CLAUDE.md` imports it with
`@AGENTS.md`, so Claude Code and other agents read the same content — put project
knowledge here, not there.

## Before you call a change done

1. `odx test /<module>` passes.
2. `odx update-module <module> -d <db>` applies cleanly on an existing database.
3. New models have access rules; new user-facing strings are translatable.
4. No changes under `src/odoo/`, and no secrets in `odoo.conf` or committed files.
